      <h2>HTML5 games, 2013</h2>
      <p>In December 2013 I taught myself HTML5 canvas by building games. Two repos survive from that run, one finished enough to play and one that never got past the title screen. Both are public domain under the Unlicense.</p>

      <h2>Furious Fruit</h2>
      <p><strong>Dec 2013</strong> &middot; <a href='https://github.com/liskl/furious_fruits'>liskl/furious_fruits</a></p>
      <p>A tutorial-based slingshot physics game, written in HTML5 canvas, CSS3 and JavaScript on top of the <strong>Box2dWeb</strong> physics engine, with jQuery for the screens. I built it with a friend who made the graphics.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What it has</strong>
          <ul class='sub'>
            <li class='sub'>A slingshot you drag back and release to launch fruit across a 640x480 canvas</li>
            <li class='sub'>Breakable wood and glass, each with its own break sound</li>
            <li class='sub'>A splash screen, level select, score counter and level complete/failed screens</li>
            <li class='sub'>Sound effects plus background music with a mute toggle</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Dec 2025 it got a second life, redeployed on the homelab Kubernetes cluster.</p>

      <h2>turf_wars</h2>
      <p><strong>Dec 2013</strong> &middot; <a href='https://github.com/liskl/turf_wars'>liskl/turf_wars</a></p>
      <p>The skeleton of an HTML5 game, built in one evening. To be honest about it: it is a stub. There is a 640x480 game container, a start screen with Singleplayer and Multiplayer options, a loading screen, and empty module files waiting for code (<code>game</code>, <code>input</code>, <code>maps</code>, <code>sp</code>). The game itself never arrived.</p>

